            {"section": "5th-schedule", "title": "Fifth Schedule — Administration of Scheduled Areas and Tribes",
             "text": "The Fifth Schedule provides for the administration and control of Scheduled Areas and Scheduled Tribes in any State other than the four northeastern States (Assam, Meghalaya, Tripura, Mizoram). The Governor of each State has special powers regarding Scheduled Areas. The Tribes Advisory Council advises the Governor on matters relating to the welfare of Scheduled Tribes."},
            {"section": "6th-schedule", "title": "Sixth Schedule — Administration of Tribal Areas in North-Eastern States",
             "text": "The Sixth Schedule provides for the administration of tribal areas in the States of Assam, Meghalaya, Tripura and Mizoram. It provides for the creation of District Councils and Regional Councils with legislative, judicial and executive powers. The Councils have the power to make laws on certain subjects including land, forests, water, agriculture, etc."},
            {"section": "7th-schedule", "title": "Seventh Schedule — Distribution of Legislative Powers",
             "text": "The Seventh Schedule contains three lists distributing legislative powers between the Union and States: (a) Union List — 97 subjects on which only Parliament can make laws (defence, foreign affairs, banking, railways, etc.); (b) State List — 66 subjects on which only State Legislatures can make laws (police, public health, agriculture, land, etc.); (c) Concurrent List — 47 subjects on which both Parliament and State Legislatures can make laws (criminal law, marriage, education, forests, etc.). In case of conflict, the Union law prevails."},
